# THE COLUMN RULES, in one docstring because all three fit in one: a TAB-indented
# line, a WHITESPACE-ONLY line, and a line indented deeper than the minimum.
# Each was wrong in the first implementation and each is wrong in a way that
# returns a plausible string rather than failing:
#   - a whitespace-only line COUNTED toward the common indent gives 2 where
#     CPython gives 6, so every other line comes out under-dedented;
#   - stripping by CHARACTER rather than by COLUMN leaves a tab-indented line
#     indented, because one tab is eight columns and one character;
#   - a partially-stripped tab must come back as SPACES -- CPython re-materialises
#     the surplus columns, so `\t\tc` under a min of 8 is eight spaces then c.
# CPython dedents docstrings at compile time (3.13+, _PyCompile_CleanDoc), so
# emitting the token's own span -- which is what the first patch did -- answers a
# wrong string for every indented docstring, lekkerzeilen/__main__.py's included.
# Diffed against CPython.
"""Summary on the first line, whose own indent is dropped outright.
	one tab, which is eight columns and sets the minimum
  
		two tabs: sixteen columns, so eight survive as SPACES
            twelve spaces: four survive
	back to the minimum
"""

print(repr(__doc__))
for _i, _ln in enumerate(__doc__.split(chr(10))):
    print(_i, repr(_ln))
